WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:15.620
My name is Alvin Smalls, owner of Lee Lee's
Baked Goods in Harlem: "Rugelach by a Brother."

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:17.420
Rugelach is a Jewish pastry.

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:19.330
It's made with cream cheese and butter dough.

00:00:19.330 --> 00:00:23.220
It has raisins, walnuts, flour, and not too
much egg.

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:26.040
In 1962, I came to New York.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:28.739
I started working in New York Hospital peeling
onions.

00:00:28.739 --> 00:00:32.250
They had a bakery there, so I went there to
work.

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:36.699
Through time I went to school and I learned
how to make a lot of Jewish pastries.

00:00:36.699 --> 00:00:39.329
Babka, hamantaschen—all this stuff I can
make.

00:00:39.329 --> 00:00:40.450
Baking is a dying art.

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:42.950
Nobody is doing it like they used to.

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:45.949
All of the bakers like myself, there's only
a few of us left.

00:00:45.949 --> 00:00:48.690
I love sweets, so I just changed everything
around.

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:53.300
I fixed it the way I like it.

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:59.879
At Lee Lee's, we make raspberry, apricot,
chocolate, and sometimes prune.

00:00:59.879 --> 00:01:03.600
The first thing you do is you mix the butter,
cream cheese, vanilla, flour, and sugar.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:07.479
You have to make the dough and let it sit
a while.

00:01:07.479 --> 00:01:10.940
In the summertime, you keep it in the refrigerator,
because the butter melts easily on the table.

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:14.760
In wintertime, it's very stiff, so you have
to take it out of the refrigerator for at

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:16.440
least half-an-hour or 45 minutes.

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:19.850
Then you roll it out, and you have to make
about 300 pieces, 200 pieces.

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:23.440
Not too thin, because if it's
too thin the food can go through.

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:28.770
Make sure you put the flour on top, fold it,
and when you roll it, it's flakey.

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:32.820
For the raisin, we usually cook it, so it
can get nice and plump and so it can stay wet

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:34.640
We add a little honey to give it flavor.

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:40.260
When you put them on top, it's moist,
so it keeps the rugelach moist inside.

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:42.540
Moist inside, flakey on the outside.

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:45.480
That's it, and then you roll it up.

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:46.800
It's a lot of process.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:50.280
Four or five other things go into it that
I can't say.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:51.980
Then you'd be better than me.

00:01:56.900 --> 00:02:00.260
Hispanic people, black people, all kinds of
people, everybody loves rugelach—everybody,

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:01.540
even the kids.

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:03.800
We had to raise the price over the years.

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:09.520
We started off at 50 cents, 70 cents, 80 cents,
a dollar, $1.25 last week.

00:02:09.539 --> 00:02:11.750
We just went up Saturday to $1.50.

00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:18.710
I didn't want to do that, but everything is
handmade—the way we put the icing on, the

00:02:18.710 --> 00:02:20.870
powdered sugar on the danish.

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:23.319
I love everything about baking, period.

00:02:23.319 --> 00:02:25.870
It's not a boring job at all.

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:26.900
I'm rolling dough.

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:30.620
I had two spinal surgeries behind it, but
it's fun.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:36.400
People come up and say, "Hey, your stuff is
good," and it makes you feel good, you know?

